// INT8 Symmetric Quantization Kernel
//
// STATUS: both kernels typecheck. `as T` is implemented as a numeric cast
// (lexer token, postfix parse rule, and a checked inference rule that
// rejects non-numeric operands and targets).
//
// Quantities (QTT):
//   input   [1]     linear, consumed exactly once
//   output  inout   linear, written in place; needs an explicit quantity
//   scale   f32     linear. It was originally [0], which is right in spirit
//                   (read-only across the loop) but wrong in practice: a [0]
//                   variable is erased, and the typechecker correctly rejects
//                   it in a runtime position. Read-only-across-the-loop is not
//                   yet expressible.
//   N is a literal (1024) because the parser requires a literal in
//   Tensor[T, N] position; a symbolic extent is not expressible yet.
//
// A `proof { .. }` block now exists: it parses, is typechecked in its own
// scope, and reaches the AST for the prover to read. `assert` is proof-only --
// using it at runtime is a type error, so it can never be a silent no-op.
//
// LOWERING AND CODEGEN (as of this commit):
//   The lowering pass now gives each `forall` a real iteration domain and a
//   real schedule band, and the WGSL backend emits loops as WGSL `for` loops.
//   This file lowers: 3 statements, 3 bands, each 1-dimensional with the
//   correct 0 <= i < 1024 domain.
//
//   It still does NOT produce a shader. `naso build --target wgsl` refuses it
//   with an explicit reason: a tensor parameter has no WGSL equivalent in a
//   function-parameter position, and `output[i] = ..` needs a buffer layout.
//   Modelling that layout is the compute-pipeline work, and is not done.
//
//   So the ladder for this file is: parses -> typechecks -> lowers to PIR with
//   correct iteration domains. It does NOT reach WGSL output, and its proof
//   obligations are still NOT discharged (float reasoning is unsupported and
//   reported as unproved, not as proved).
//
// Still not implemented:
//   1. Obligation discharge for floats. naso-verify DOES read proof blocks and
//      discharges the obligations it can encode, but the SMT layer has no
//      floating-point sort, so BOTH assertions here are reported
//      NASO-OBL-002 "not discharged" at Warning severity. They are neither
//      confirmed nor refuted. Encoding f32 as Real would silently prove a
//      different, weaker claim, so the prover refuses instead.
//   2. Symbolic extents (see N above).
//
// `forall` is a proposition in expression position and a loop in statement
// position. A proof block is erased, so reading `input` in an obligation does
// not consume it -- which is what lets an obligation be stated about a `[1]`
// linear value at all.
//
// The per-element precondition that makes the i8 cast sound is now *stated*,
// above, and the prover now reads proof blocks. But these particular
// obligations are NOT established: they are floating-point, which the SMT
// encoding cannot represent, so they come back as NASO-OBL-002 rather than as
// proofs. So the clamp remains load-bearing rather than decorative -- it is
// what keeps the narrowing in range if the precondition is violated at
// runtime, and it is also what makes the operation lossy.
//
// Integer obligations of the same shape ARE discharged; see
// crates/naso-verify/src/prover/obligations.rs.

/// Quantize f32 -> i8 with a symmetric scale.
///
/// The cast can overflow unless the precondition holds, which is what a
/// proof should establish:
///
///   forall i in 0..1024 { abs(input[i] / scale) <= 127.0 }
///
/// This obligation is stated but not discharged (see the file header). The
/// clamp is therefore load-bearing rather than decorative: it is what keeps
/// the narrowing in range if the precondition is violated, and it is also
/// what makes the operation lossy.
fn quantize_int8_symmetric(
    input: [1] Tensor[f32, 1024],
    output: inout [1] Tensor[i8, 1024],
    scale: f32
) {
    proof {
        assert(scale > 0.0);
        assert(forall i in 0..1024 { abs(input[i] / scale) <= 127.0 });
    }

    forall i in 0..1024 {
        let v = round(input[i] / scale);
        output[i] = clamp(v, -128.0, 127.0) as i8;
    }
}

/// Dequantize i8 -> f32, widening each element and rescaling.
fn dequantize_int8_symmetric(
    input: [1] Tensor[i8, 1024],
    output: inout [1] Tensor[f32, 1024],
    scale: f32
) {
    forall i in 0..1024 {
        let v = input[i] as f32;
        output[i] = v * scale;
    }
}

/// f32 -> f32 using the same prelude intrinsics a quantizer needs, with no
/// cast involved. Useful as a smoke test that forall loops, tensor indexing
/// and the math builtins all typecheck.
fn normalize_f32(
    input: [1] Tensor[f32, 1024],
    output: inout [1] Tensor[f32, 1024],
    scale: f32
) {
    forall i in 0..1024 {
        output[i] = round(input[i] / scale);
    }
}
